# dependencies
node_modules/

# package manager local store
.pnpm-store/

# app runtime data (search index, history, trash)
.nekowite/
.nekowite-trash/

# Daily notes the app writes when this repo is opened as a vault (it happened
# once and a stray empty note got committed): runtime output, not source.
/daily/

# build output
dist/
target/
src-tauri/target/

# The ACL permissions `tauri-build` autogenerates from `src-tauri/build.rs`'s app manifest, one
# `allow-<command>`/`deny-<command>` pair per declared command. Derived, and regenerated before
# they are read on every build — so a clone without them builds. The directory itself is NOT
# ignored: `src-tauri/permissions/` is where a hand-written app permission would live, and only
# the autogenerated half is a build product.
apps/desktop/src-tauri/permissions/autogenerated/

# test / coverage artifacts
test-results/
playwright-report/
coverage/

# local ephemeral files
*.local

# Real-device probe harness + the throwaway vaults its runs create.
#
# DO NOT ADD THIS DIRECTORY TO THE REPOSITORY. The live-AI probes carry a real
# API key for the endpoint they exercise, so committing it would publish a
# working credential. Keep the ignore rule and keep the key out of git.
apps/desktop/ai-lab/
/probe-*/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# local release build artifact
/nekowite-bin
/release/

# Local package-manager config. A registry mirror is a developer's own
# network choice, not a project decision: committing one forces every
# contributor and CI through a third-party mirror outside their control.
.npmrc

# editor / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo

# The Windows development clone. Its history was merged into this repository
# (see the "integrate the NekoWite_win line" merge), so the directory is now a
# redundant second checkout with its own .git. Tracking it would commit a
# gitlink to a commit this repository cannot resolve. Keep the working copy
# locally, but never add it.
/NekoWite_win/

# Linked worktrees (`git worktree add`). Each is a full checkout of another
# branch, so git sees the directory as untracked and would happily commit a
# nested repository. They are scratch: the commits live on their own branches.
/.worktrees/

# Third-party reference checkouts. Each is a full clone carrying its own `.git`,
# so git would commit a gitlink to a commit this repository cannot resolve (and,
# for the large ones, tens of megabytes of vendored source). They are read-only
# study material: never a build input, never a sidecar, never a release bundle.
# `/zed-main/` is the ACP design reference; `/references/` holds the others.
/references/
/zed-main/

# Pinned DesktopPet source used for the Linux port. Keep the nested clone out
# of commits; build/package inputs must also exclude this reference directory.
/references/desktop-pet/

# The bundled OpenCode engine (scripts/fetch-opencode-linux.sh installs it here).
# 176 MB per release: it belongs in the packaging pipeline's output, not in a
# commit. The path is what Tauri's `bundle.externalBin` resolves, so it must
# exist to build a bundle — and must never be tracked.
/apps/desktop/src-tauri/binaries/

# P0 scratch: the throwaway OpenCode profile and probe scripts for the ACP
# compatibility work. The profile must live inside the repo (plan §3.2) and must
# never be committed — it is a test identity, not project state.
/.tmp-p0/

# Profile-isolation scratch: decoy configuration planted in every directory the
# engine's discovery looks at, plus the throwaway profile trees the two-instance
# measurement plants under `.tmp-isolation-run/<label>` — one parent, one label per
# test run, so both tests share this rule instead of adding a rule each. Same
# reasoning as above — a decoy tree is evidence, not project state.
/.tmp-isolation*/

# The two-instance reachability harness and its phase logs (`.tmp-instances/`):
# `run-phase.sh`, the probes' raw output, and whatever a phase starts. Kept so the
# report that cites them can be checked, and never committed — the harness is not
# part of the suite, and its logs are a measurement, not project state.
/.tmp-instances/

# The write-refusal experiment's scratch: a real engine launched against a
# throwaway profile, so what lands here is the engine's OWN tree — its database,
# its model cache, and the per-session snapshot directories it writes on every
# run. Kept on failure as the evidence the test's report points at, and cleaned
# on success. Never committed, for the reason every rule above gives: the engine's
# working state is not this project's state, and a snapshot directory is not a
# source file. It grows with each failed run, so a stray copy is also a large one.
/.tmp-fs-write/

# The permission-grants surface's scratch: the profile tree and the engine database a
# live grants run writes through, so the readout is measured against the engine that
# produced it rather than against a fixture. Never committed, for the reason every rule
# above gives — the engine's working state is not this project's state.
/.tmp-permission-grants/
